---
id: com.example.world-clock
name: World Clock
version: 0.1.0
---

A Composer plugin that adds a world clock to every space — a map with a row of clocks — built in a sandbox on EDGE
and published to its reader's private plugin registry, where they install it.

## Extensions

The following extension dialects are used in this document.
Each extension is defined in the Appendix or resolved via its URI.

| Term       | URI                          |
| ---------- | ---------------------------- |
| `type`     | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`      |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`      |
| `scenario` | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`  |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0`  |

## Types

```mdl
type City
  desc: One clock: a timezone and where it is, from `timezones` in `@dxos/react-ui-geo/data`.
  fields:
    timezone: string       # an IANA name, `Asia/Tokyo`
    position: [number, number]
```

```mdl
type Clock
  desc: A space's clocks, one object per space, stored on its first change.
  fields:
    cities: City[]
```

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Clocks page
  desc: A "World Clock" group in each space's navtree with a "Clocks" page under it, showing a single row of clock cards.

  req F-1.1: The row is sorted west to east, and each card shows the date, the time (24-hour, zero-padded, no AM/PM, ticking every second) and its timezone.
  req F-1.2: Until the space's `type:Clock` is first changed, the page shows one card for the reader's own timezone.
  req F-1.3: Each clock card carries `data-testid="worldClock.clock"`, the empty card after the row `data-testid="worldClock.new"`, its plus button `data-testid="worldClock.add"` and each card's delete button `data-testid="worldClock.delete"`.
  req F-1.4: The plugin adds to the navtree and never replaces it: no workspace, no rail tab.
```

F-2 to F-4 are named on the project's feature map — Map, Add and remove clocks, Published to the registry — and are
not written yet. Each gets a `feat` block here, with its requirements, before the implementation is planned.

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: The Clocks page opens on the reader's own time
  when: a reader opens Clocks under the World Clock group of a space with no clocks yet
  then: the page shows one clock card, for the reader's own timezone, and its seconds tick
```

